Lesson 3 — Acknowledgement, Delivery Semantics & Redelivery
This is one of the most important lessons in distributed communication.
If you truly understand today's topic, you'll understand why systems like Kafka, RabbitMQ, SQS, and many internal event platforms make seemingly strange design choices.
The central problem is:
How does a broker know that a consumer has successfully processed a message?
And more importantly:
What happens when something fails between "received" and "processed"?
________________________________________
1. Start with the basic lifecycle
Suppose we have:
Producer
   |
   | M1
   v
Broker
   |
   | M1
   v
Consumer
A naive lifecycle looks like:
SEND
 ↓
RECEIVE
 ↓
PROCESS
 ↓
DONE
But the broker cannot directly observe:
PROCESS
It only knows things such as:
"I sent M1 to Consumer A."

"Consumer A acknowledged M1."
Therefore we introduce an explicit acknowledgement:
Broker ── M1 ──> Consumer
Broker <── ACK ── Consumer
________________________________________
2. What does ACK actually mean?
An ACK essentially says:
"You may consider this delivery successfully handled according to our messaging protocol."
But what exactly "handled" means is a contract.
It might mean:
Message reached application
or:
Application successfully processed message
or:
Application persisted the result
These are very different guarantees.
This is why when designing a messaging system, never casually say:
"We acknowledge the message."
Instead ask:
At what point do we acknowledge it?
________________________________________
3. The dangerous implementation
Imagine:
Message message = consumer.receive();

consumer.ack(message);

process(message);
This looks efficient.
But consider:
receive
  ↓
ACK
  ↓
process
  ↓
CRASH
The broker believes:
M1 = successfully handled
But the business operation never happened.
Result:
M1 LOST
Therefore:
Acknowledging before processing gives you an at-most-once style failure mode.
________________________________________
4. The opposite implementation
Now do:
Message message = consumer.receive();

process(message);

consumer.ack(message);
Better.
Sequence:
receive
  ↓
process
  ↓
ACK
Now imagine:
process()
  ↓
SUCCESS
  ↓
CRASH
  ↓
ACK never happens
Broker sees:
M1 = unacknowledged
So:
M1
 ↓
redelivery
 ↓
Consumer receives M1 again
Now:
process(M1)
runs twice.
This gives us the fundamental trade-off.
________________________________________
5. At-most-once
At-most-once means:
A message is processed zero or one times.
Conceptually:
receive
  ↓
ACK
  ↓
process
Failure:
ACK
 ↓
CRASH
 ↓
processing never occurs
Message is lost.
So:
At-most-once

No duplicates
but
possible loss
This can be acceptable for things like:
•	metrics
•	non-critical telemetry
•	best-effort notifications
•	ephemeral events
depending on requirements.
________________________________________
6. At-least-once
Now:
receive
  ↓
process
  ↓
ACK
If processing succeeds but ACK fails:
process
  ↓
SUCCESS
  ↓
network failure
  ↓
ACK doesn't reach broker
Broker thinks:
M1 = unprocessed
and redelivers:
M1 → Consumer
So:
At-least-once

Possible duplicates
but
message loss is minimized
This is extremely common in real distributed systems.
________________________________________
7. Why at-least-once is often preferred
Consider payment processing.
Would you rather have:
Option A:
payment event disappears
or:
Option B:
payment event gets processed twice
Neither is good.
But Option B can potentially be controlled using idempotency.
For example:
paymentId = PAY-123
Consumer:
if paymentAlreadyProcessed("PAY-123"):
    return;

processPayment();

markProcessed("PAY-123");
Now duplicate delivery doesn't necessarily produce a duplicate business effect.
Therefore a very common architecture is:
At-least-once delivery
+
Idempotent consumer
________________________________________
8. Exactly-once
People often describe exactly-once as:
Message processed exactly once
But this statement hides enormous complexity.
Consider:
Broker
  |
  | M1
  v
Consumer
  |
  v
Database
Consumer does:
UPDATE account
SET balance = balance - 100;
Database commits.
Then:
Consumer crashes
before ACK.
Broker redelivers M1.
Consumer performs:
UPDATE account
SET balance = balance - 100;
again.
You have:
-100
-100
The message wasn't necessarily delivered incorrectly.
The problem is:
The business side effect wasn't coordinated with acknowledgement.
________________________________________
9. The classic distributed transaction problem
This is worth memorizing.
We have:
Message Broker
      |
      v
Consumer
      |
      v
Database
The desired atomic operation is:
process message
+
commit database
+
ack message
as one indivisible transaction.
But these are typically separate systems:
Broker transaction
        ≠
Database transaction
So consider:
Step 1: receive M1
Step 2: update DB
Step 3: DB commit
Step 4: ACK broker
Failure after Step 3:
DB = committed
Broker = unacknowledged
Redelivery occurs.
________________________________________
10. Reverse ordering doesn't solve it
Maybe you say:
Step 1: receive
Step 2: ACK
Step 3: update DB
Now failure after Step 2:
Broker = acknowledged
DB = not updated
Message is lost from the consumer's perspective.
So:
ACK → DB
can lose work.
While:
DB → ACK
can duplicate work.
This is a fundamental problem.
________________________________________
11. Why idempotency matters
The usual solution is to make processing safe to repeat.
Suppose:
M1 = PaymentCompleted(paymentId=123)
Consumer receives M1.
Before processing:
ProcessedPayments
-----------------
123
doesn't exist.
Consumer performs:
BEGIN TRANSACTION

INSERT paymentId=123 into ProcessedPayments

UPDATE order SET status='PAID'

COMMIT
Now:
ProcessedPayments
-----------------
123
If M1 arrives again:
SELECT paymentId=123
finds it.
Therefore:
Already processed
       ↓
Don't repeat side effect
The important point is that deduplication state and the business update should ideally be committed atomically in the same database transaction.
________________________________________
12. Idempotency mathematically
An operation f is idempotent if:
f(f(x)) = f(x)
Example:
SET order.status = PAID
Repeated:
PAID → PAID
is harmless.
But:
balance = balance - 100
is not naturally idempotent.
Repeated:
1000 → 900 → 800
changes the result.
Therefore we often transform non-idempotent operations into idempotent ones using a unique operation ID.
For example:
paymentId = 123
Then:
process payment 123
means:
apply operation 123 exactly once
from the application's perspective.
________________________________________
13. Redelivery
Let's look at how redelivery can happen.
Case A — Consumer crashes
Broker
  |
  | M1
  v
Consumer
  |
  X
CRASH
No ACK.
Broker eventually:
M1 → available
and:
M1 → Consumer 2
________________________________________
Case B — ACK timeout
Broker → M1 → Consumer
Consumer processes:
3 seconds
But broker expects ACK within:
2 seconds
So:
t=0    M1 delivered
t=2    timeout
t=2    M1 redelivered
t=3    Consumer 1 finishes
Now both consumers may be processing M1.
This is why timeout configuration matters.
________________________________________
14. Redelivery doesn't necessarily mean failure
This is a subtle point.
Suppose:
Consumer A receives M1
and takes 20 seconds.
Broker's visibility timeout:
10 seconds
At t=10:
M1 → Consumer B
Consumer A is not necessarily dead.
It might simply be slow.
So now:
Consumer A → M1
Consumer B → M1
This creates concurrent duplicate processing.
Therefore production consumers should often be designed to tolerate duplicates.
________________________________________
15. Retry policy
Suppose processing fails:
M1
 ↓
Consumer
 ↓
FAIL
What should happen?
Naively:
retry immediately
retry immediately
retry immediately
retry immediately
...
This is dangerous.
Imagine the downstream database is unavailable.
You could create:
M1 → fail
M1 → fail
M1 → fail
M1 → fail
at extremely high speed.
This creates a retry storm.
________________________________________
16. Exponential backoff
Instead:
Attempt 1 → fail
wait 1 sec

Attempt 2 → fail
wait 2 sec

Attempt 3 → fail
wait 4 sec

Attempt 4 → fail
wait 8 sec

Attempt 5 → fail
wait 16 sec
Conceptually:
delay = base × 2^attempt
Usually with jitter.
For example:
delay = random(0, calculatedBackoff)
Why jitter?
Imagine 10,000 consumers all fail at exactly 12:00:00.
Without jitter:
12:00:01 → 10,000 retries
12:00:03 → 10,000 retries
12:00:07 → 10,000 retries
With jitter:
12:00:00.3 → some
12:00:00.8 → some
12:00:01.4 → some
...
Load gets spread out.
________________________________________
17. Poison messages
Now consider:
M1 = malformed message
Every consumer attempt fails:
M1
 ↓
FAIL
 ↓
retry
 ↓
FAIL
 ↓
retry
 ↓
FAIL
If you endlessly retry:
M1
can block useful work or consume huge resources.
This is a poison message.
________________________________________
18. Dead Letter Queue
After some number of attempts:
Attempt 1 ❌
Attempt 2 ❌
Attempt 3 ❌
Attempt 4 ❌
Attempt 5 ❌
move it to:
Dead Letter Queue
Architecture:
             ┌─────────────┐
             │ Main Queue  │
             └──────┬──────┘
                    |
                    v
                Consumer
                    |
              ┌─────┴─────┐
              |           |
           success       failure
              |           |
             ACK          |
                          retry
                            |
                         max attempts
                            |
                            v
                         DLQ
DLQ lets operators investigate:
Why did this message fail?
without blocking normal processing.
________________________________________
19. But DLQ isn't magic
A bad design is:
retry 3 times
→ DLQ
→ forget
Because DLQ messages may represent:
lost business operations
A good system has:
DLQ
 ↓
Monitoring
 ↓
Alert
 ↓
Investigation
 ↓
Fix
 ↓
Replay
If using a log-based system, replay can be especially natural.
________________________________________
20. Retry classification
Not every error should be retried.
Consider:
HTTP 500
Maybe retry.
HTTP 503
Probably retry.
But:
Invalid customer ID
Retrying won't fix it.
Likewise:
Malformed JSON
should generally not be retried indefinitely.
So classify errors:
Transient error
    ↓
Retry

Permanent error
    ↓
DLQ / reject

Unknown error
    ↓
Limited retries + investigation
This distinction is extremely important operationally.
________________________________________
21. Acknowledgement timing
There are several possible points where the consumer can ACK.
Option A
receive
 ↓
ACK
 ↓
process
Fast but unsafe.
Option B
receive
 ↓
process
 ↓
ACK
Safer, but duplicates are possible.
Option C
receive
 ↓
DB transaction
 ↓
commit
 ↓
ACK
Common pattern when using an external DB, provided processing is idempotent/transactionally deduplicated.
Option D
Use a transaction spanning message consumption and processing where the messaging platform supports an appropriate transactional model.
This can provide stronger semantics, but is more complex and does not automatically make arbitrary external side effects exactly-once.
________________________________________
22. The "exactly once" trap
Suppose:
Kafka → Consumer → Database
Even if Kafka supports sophisticated transactional semantics, imagine the consumer does:
sendSMS()
to an external provider.
Now:
SMS Provider
is another system.
You cannot casually claim:
Kafka transaction
+
SMS
=
exactly once
The external provider needs its own idempotency mechanism or transaction boundary.
This is a general principle:
Exactly-once semantics do not magically propagate across system boundaries.
________________________________________
23. Producer-side duplicates
We've mostly talked about consumer duplicates.
But producers can create duplicates too.
Consider:
Producer → Broker
Producer sends:
M1
Broker receives it.
But response gets lost:
Broker
  |
  | SUCCESS
  X
Producer
Producer thinks:
"Maybe broker never received M1."
So it retries:
M1 again
Broker could now have:
M1
M1
Therefore duplicate prevention may be required on both sides:
Producer
   |
   | retries
   v
Broker
   |
   | redelivery
   v
Consumer
________________________________________
24. Idempotent producer
A sophisticated broker protocol may allow the producer to identify retries.
Conceptually:
producerId = P1
sequence = 42
First:
P1, 42, M1
Retry:
P1, 42, M1
Broker recognizes:
same producer + same sequence
and avoids treating it as a new message.
This is one technique for reducing producer-side duplication.
Again, exact semantics depend on the messaging system.
________________________________________
25. Delivery semantics across the whole pipeline
Here's the picture you should memorize:
              Producer
                 |
                 | retry
                 v
              Broker
                 |
                 | redelivery
                 v
              Consumer
                 |
                 | retry
                 v
             Database/API
Duplicates can arise at multiple boundaries.
Therefore:
"Exactly once" 
is not simply a property of the broker.
It's an end-to-end system property.
________________________________________
26. A serious system-design example
Let's design:
Order → Payment
Requirements:
•	Payment must not happen twice.
•	Broker can redeliver.
•	Consumer can crash.
•	Payment provider can timeout.
•	Payment provider supports idempotency keys.
Architecture:
             PaymentRequested
                    |
                    v
              Payment Queue
                    |
                    v
             Payment Worker
                    |
                    | idempotencyKey=orderId
                    v
             Payment Provider
                    |
                    v
               Payment DB
Worker:
receive(message)

paymentId = message.paymentId

result = paymentProvider.charge(
    idempotencyKey = paymentId
)

saveResult(result)

ACK(message)
Suppose worker crashes after charge:
charge SUCCESS
     |
     X
   crash
Message is redelivered:
PaymentRequested
      |
      v
charge(idempotencyKey=123)
Payment provider responds:
Already processed.
Return existing result.
No second charge.
This is a very strong practical pattern.
________________________________________
27. Ordering + redelivery
Now combine today's concepts with ordering.
Suppose:
Order 100:

M1 = CREATED
M2 = PAID
M3 = SHIPPED
We require:
M1 → M2 → M3
But M1 fails:
M1 → Consumer → FAIL
What should happen to M2?
If M2 is processed before M1 succeeds:
M2 → PAID
M1 → CREATED
we have broken logical ordering.
Therefore ordering often requires serialization per entity.
For example:
partitionKey = orderId
Then:
Order 100
    |
    v
Partition 7

M1
M2
M3
and a single consumer processes them in sequence.
________________________________________
28. But this creates a trade-off
Ordering requires:
M1 → M2 → M3
Parallelism wants:
M1
M2
M3
processed concurrently.
You cannot generally have unlimited parallelism and strict global ordering simultaneously.
Therefore we often compromise:
Preserve ordering only where business semantics require it.
Examples:
Same order → ordered
Different orders → parallel
This is an excellent system-design answer.
________________________________________
29. What happens when a consumer is slow?
Suppose:
Producer = 100K/sec
Consumer = 20K/sec
Lag:
80K/sec
You have several choices.
Scale consumers
C1
C2
C3
C4
C5
But this only works if the queue has enough partitions/parallelism.
Increase partitions
If:
2 partitions
but you want:
10 consumers
you may not be able to utilize all consumers effectively.
A common principle:
Parallelism is bounded by the number of independently consumable partitions/shards.
________________________________________
30. Another important failure: consumer stuck on one message
Suppose:
M1 = poison message
M2 = valid
M3 = valid
Strict FIFO processing:
M1 ❌
could prevent:
M2
M3
from proceeding.
This creates a tension between:
strict ordering
and:
availability / throughput
Possible solutions include:
•	retry M1 with backoff
•	move M1 to DLQ after limits
•	partition by entity so unrelated entities continue
•	allow parallel processing where ordering isn't required
________________________________________
31. The distributed-systems rule emerging
By now you should notice a pattern.
Every guarantee has a cost.
More durability
    ↓
more replication / I/O
More ordering
    ↓
less parallelism
More retries
    ↓
more duplicate processing
More aggressive retry
    ↓
more load during failure
At-least-once
    ↓
need idempotency
Longer retention
    ↓
more storage
This is exactly how you should reason in system design.
________________________________________
32. The failure table you should be able to explain
Scenario	Result
ACK before processing	Possible message loss
Processing before ACK	Possible duplicate
Consumer crashes before ACK	Redelivery
ACK lost	Redelivery
Producer response lost	Producer retry / duplicate
Processing takes longer than lease	Possible concurrent redelivery
Permanent processing error	DLQ after retry policy
Consumer slower than producer	Growing lag
Broker loses unreplicated data	Possible message loss
Partition failure	Depends on replication/failover
Duplicate message	Requires idempotent handling
If you can explain why each happens, you're beyond memorizing messaging terminology.
