Lesson 4 — Ordering, Partitioning & Consumer Groups
Now we're getting into the part of distributed messaging that directly connects to scalability.
The central tension is:
How do we process millions of messages concurrently while still preserving the ordering that the business actually cares about?
The answer is usually:
Partitioning + consumer groups + carefully chosen partition keys.
________________________________________
1. Start with one queue
Imagine one queue:
Producer
   |
   v
+----------------------+
| Queue                |
|                      |
| M1 M2 M3 M4 M5 M6    |
+----------------------+
   |
   v
Consumer
This gives us a simple property:
M1 → M2 → M3 → M4
But there is a problem.
Throughput is limited
One consumer might process:
10,000 msg/sec
But your system receives:
1,000,000 msg/sec
You need parallelism.
________________________________________
2. Add multiple consumers
You might try:
                  ┌── Consumer 1
Queue ────────────┼── Consumer 2
                  ├── Consumer 3
                  └── Consumer 4
Now the broker distributes messages:
M1 → C1
M2 → C2
M3 → C3
M4 → C4
Great.
But what happens to ordering?
Suppose processing times are:
C1: M1 = 100ms
C2: M2 = 10ms
C3: M3 = 50ms
Completion could be:
M2
M3
M1
So although delivery may have been:
M1 → M2 → M3
processing completion isn't ordered.
________________________________________
3. Why global ordering is expensive
Suppose you demand:
M1 → M2 → M3 → M4
must execute strictly in that order.
Then:
M2 cannot begin
until M1 finishes.
And:
M3 cannot begin
until M2 finishes.
So:
M1
 ↓
M2
 ↓
M3
 ↓
M4
You've essentially serialized the workload.
Your 1,000 consumers aren't useful if only one message can be processed at a time.
Therefore:
Strict global ordering and massive parallelism are fundamentally in tension.
________________________________________
4. Business ordering is usually narrower
Fortunately, most systems don't need global ordering.
Consider orders:
Order A:
Created → Paid → Shipped

Order B:
Created → Paid → Shipped

Order C:
Created → Paid → Shipped
Do we really require:
A.Created
A.Paid
A.Shipped
B.Created
B.Paid
B.Shipped
C.Created
...
Probably not.
We only need:
A.Created → A.Paid → A.Shipped
and:
B.Created → B.Paid → B.Shipped
and:
C.Created → C.Paid → C.Shipped
Now we can process different orders concurrently.
This is the fundamental idea behind partitioning.
________________________________________
5. Partitioning
Instead of one queue:
Queue
we create:
Topic
 |
 +── Partition 0
 +── Partition 1
 +── Partition 2
 +── Partition 3
Each partition is independently ordered.
Example:
P0:
A1 A2 A3 A4

P1:
B1 B2 B3

P2:
C1 C2 C3
Within P0:
A1 → A2 → A3 → A4
ordering is preserved.
Across P0 and P1:
A1 ? B1
there is no meaningful global ordering guarantee.
________________________________________
6. Partition key
Now the most important question:
How do we decide which partition receives a message?
Usually:
partition = hash(partitionKey) % N
For example:
partitionKey = orderId
Suppose:
orderId = 101
hash(101) % 4 = 2
Then:
Order 101 → Partition 2
Another event:
Order 101 → Partition 2
Again.
Therefore:
P2:

OrderCreated(101)
PaymentCompleted(101)
OrderShipped(101)
This gives us per-order ordering.
________________________________________
7. Why orderId is often a good key
Suppose the requirement is:
Events belonging to the same order must be processed in order.
Then:
partitionKey = orderId
is natural.
But suppose your requirement is:
All events from a user must be ordered.
Then:
partitionKey = userId
is more appropriate.
The partition key should come from the ordering requirement, not from a random technical choice.
A very useful interview sentence is:
"I'll choose the partition key based on the entity for which ordering is semantically required."
________________________________________
8. Example: banking
Suppose:
Account 123
generates:
Deposit +100
Withdraw -50
Deposit +200
You may require:
+100 → -50 → +200
because account balance transitions depend on sequence.
Therefore:
partitionKey = accountId
Then:
Account 123 → Partition 7
All account events stay together.
Meanwhile:
Account 456 → Partition 3
can process concurrently.
________________________________________
9. Consumer groups
Now we have:
Topic
 |
 +── P0
 +── P1
 +── P2
 +── P3
Suppose we create a consumer group:
PaymentWorkers
 |
 +── C1
 +── C2
 +── C3
 +── C4
The system assigns partitions:
P0 → C1
P1 → C2
P2 → C3
P3 → C4
So each partition is processed by one consumer in that group at a time.
________________________________________
10. Why consumer groups exist
Suppose you have:
10 partitions
and:
10 consumers
You can process:
10 partitions concurrently
This is horizontal scaling.
Instead of:
1 consumer
10K msg/sec
you can have:
10 consumers
~100K msg/sec
assuming processing and infrastructure scale appropriately.
________________________________________
11. Consumer count > partition count
Now suppose:
Partitions = 4
Consumers = 10
You cannot give every consumer its own partition.
You get something like:
P0 → C1
P1 → C2
P2 → C3
P3 → C4

C5 → idle
C6 → idle
C7 → idle
C8 → idle
C9 → idle
Therefore:
Within a consumer group, useful active parallelism is bounded by the number of partitions.
This is an important capacity-planning concept.
________________________________________
12. Consumer count < partition count
Now:
Partitions = 8
Consumers = 4
You might get:
C1 → P0, P4
C2 → P1, P5
C3 → P2, P6
C4 → P3, P7
Each consumer processes multiple partitions.
So:
Consumers < Partitions
is perfectly valid.
________________________________________
13. Adding consumers
Suppose initially:
P0 → C1
P1 → C2
P2 → C3
P3 → C4
Now add:
C5
The system needs to redistribute partitions:
P0 → C1
P1 → C2
P2 → C3
P3 → C5
or some other allocation.
This redistribution is called a:
Rebalance
________________________________________
14. Why rebalancing exists
Consumers can dynamically appear/disappear.
Example:
C1
C2
C3
C2 crashes:
C1
C2 ❌
C3
Its partitions need reassignment.
Otherwise:
P1
would have nobody processing it.
So the group coordinator detects membership changes and performs a rebalance.
Conceptually:
Consumer failure
      ↓
Detect membership change
      ↓
Reassign partitions
      ↓
Consumers resume processing
________________________________________
15. Rebalancing has a cost
Rebalancing isn't free.
During some rebalance strategies, consumers may temporarily stop processing.
So:
Consumer churn
     ↓
Frequent rebalances
     ↓
Processing pauses
     ↓
Latency increases
This is why unstable consumers can cause serious production problems.
For example:
C1 joins
C2 joins
C3 joins
C2 crashes
C2 restarts
C3 crashes
...
could produce continuous reassignment.
________________________________________
16. Partition assignment
A group coordinator needs an algorithm to decide:
Which consumer gets which partition?
Conceptually, it tries to distribute:
Partitions = 6
Consumers = 3
as evenly as possible:
C1 → P0 P3
C2 → P1 P4
C3 → P2 P5
Exact assignment strategies vary between messaging systems.
At system-design level, understand the invariant:
Each partition should normally have one active consumer within a consumer group.
________________________________________
17. Multiple consumer groups
Now something powerful.
Suppose:
OrderEvents
needs to be consumed by:
Payment
Inventory
Analytics
Notifications
Don't create one giant consumer group.
Create independent groups:
                 OrderEvents
                     |
        ┌────────────┼────────────┐
        ↓            ↓            ↓
     Payment      Inventory    Analytics
      Group         Group         Group
Each group tracks its own position.
So:
Payment:
offset = 500

Analytics:
offset = 450

Inventory:
offset = 499
They are independent.
________________________________________
18. Why this is powerful
Suppose Analytics is broken for 30 minutes.
Analytics ❌
Payment can continue:
Payment ✓
Inventory:
Inventory ✓
When Analytics returns:
Analytics
    ↓
resume from previous offset
This gives consumer isolation.
One application's slowness doesn't inherently force another application to stop consuming the same event stream.
________________________________________
19. Queue vs consumer group mental model
A traditional work queue often means:
M1
 |
 +--> Worker A OR Worker B
One worker gets the work.
A log with consumer groups is more like:
              Event
                |
       ┌────────┼────────┐
       ↓        ↓        ↓
   Group A   Group B   Group C
       |        |        |
      C1       C2       C3
The same event can be processed independently by multiple applications.
This is why event streaming and traditional task queues should not be mentally conflated.
________________________________________
20. Hot partitions
Now for a major real-world problem.
Suppose:
10 partitions
and you use:
partitionKey = userId
Normally:
P0 █████
P1 █████
P2 █████
P3 █████
...
Good distribution.
But suppose one user is extremely active:
Celebrity user = 100 million events
Then:
P7 ███████████████████████████████
P0 ██
P1 ██
P2 ██
...
P7 becomes a hot partition.
Adding more consumers doesn't necessarily fix it:
P7 → C7
Only one consumer may process P7 at a time.
________________________________________
21. How do you fix a hot partition?
One approach is to add a secondary shard component.
Instead of:
partitionKey = userId
use something like:
partitionKey = userId + shardNumber
For example:
celebrityUser-0
celebrityUser-1
celebrityUser-2
celebrityUser-3
Now events can spread across partitions.
But you've potentially sacrificed strict ordering for that user.
This is another fundamental trade-off:
Strict ordering
       ↕
High parallelism
You must decide which one the business actually needs.
________________________________________
22. A more subtle ordering problem
Suppose:
Order 123
has:
Created
Paid
Shipped
All go to P2.
Great.
But now:
Created → Consumer C1
Paid    → Consumer C1
Shipped → Consumer C1
C1 crashes while processing Paid.
What happens?
Paid → redelivery
You must ensure that Shipped isn't incorrectly processed before Paid has succeeded if business semantics require strict sequencing.
So partitioning gives you ordered storage, but the application still has to respect the processing semantics.
________________________________________
23. Ordering isn't the same as completion
This is worth emphasizing.
Suppose a partition contains:
M1 M2 M3
and the consumer fetches them.
If application code parallelizes:
Thread 1 → M1
Thread 2 → M2
Thread 3 → M3
then you may get:
M2 completes
M3 completes
M1 completes
The partition's order was preserved at the storage/delivery level, but application-level execution order was not.
Therefore:
If ordering matters, don't introduce parallel processing inside a partition without explicitly designing for ordered execution.
________________________________________
24. Partition count is a capacity decision
Suppose you create:
2 partitions
today.
Later:
Traffic = 1 million msg/sec
and you want:
100 consumers
Two partitions limit useful consumer parallelism.
So partition count should be designed with future scalability in mind.
But don't blindly create millions of partitions either.
Partitions consume:
•	broker metadata
•	file descriptors/resources
•	memory
•	replication bandwidth
•	coordination overhead
So there is a capacity-planning trade-off.
________________________________________
25. A useful sizing equation
At a simplified level:
Required consumer parallelism
≈ incoming throughput / per-consumer throughput
Example:
Incoming = 100,000 msg/sec
One consumer = 5,000 msg/sec
Then:
100,000 / 5,000 = 20
You need roughly:
20 active processing units
Therefore you need sufficient partition-level parallelism to support that.
This is a simplified model; real systems must also account for message size, downstream latency, batching, skew, replication, and headroom.
________________________________________
26. Partition ordering is local
Suppose:
P0:
A1 A2 A3

P1:
B1 B2 B3
Guaranteed:
A1 < A2 < A3
B1 < B2 < B3
Not necessarily:
A1 < B1
This is why if someone in a system-design interview says:
"We need ordering."
Your next question should be:
"Ordering across what scope?"
Possible answers:
•	globally
•	per customer
•	per account
•	per order
•	per device
•	per partition
That question is often more important than the choice of broker.
________________________________________
27. Design example — banking transactions
Let's apply everything.
Requirements:
100 million accounts

Each account generates:
Deposit
Withdrawal
Transfer
Interest
Need:
Transactions for the same account must be processed in logical order.
But:
Different accounts should process concurrently.
A good high-level design:
                 Transaction Events
                         |
                         v
                    Partitioner
                         |
          ┌──────────────┼──────────────┐
          ↓              ↓              ↓
         P0             P1             P2
          |              |              |
         C1             C2             C3
Partition key:
accountId
Therefore:
Account A → P7
Account B → P3
Account C → P1
Within each account:
A1 → A2 → A3
Different accounts:
A1 ──────┐
B1 ──────┼── parallel
C1 ──────┘
This gives us the desired combination:
Per-account ordering
+
Cross-account parallelism
________________________________________
28. But what about transfers?
Now it gets interesting.
Suppose:
Transfer ₹500
Account A → Account B
We have two entities:
A
B
Which partition owns the event?
If partitioned by:
sourceAccountId
then:
Transfer → Partition(A)
But B's balance is also affected.
Now we have a distributed consistency problem.
You can't simply say:
partitionKey = accountId
and assume the problem disappears.
For operations involving multiple ordered entities, you need to carefully define:
•	transaction boundaries
•	ordering requirements
•	consistency requirements
•	concurrency model
This is where messaging starts connecting to distributed transactions and event-driven architecture.
________________________________________
29. Rebalancing + failure scenario
Let's put everything together.
Initial:
P0 → C1
P1 → C2
P2 → C3
P3 → C4
C2 crashes.
C2 ❌
Coordinator detects it.
Then:
P1 → C1
perhaps.
But what happens to messages C2 was processing?
Suppose:
P1:
M10 M11 M12 M13

C2:
processing M12
C2 crashes before acknowledging/committing its progress.
After reassignment:
C1:
resume from last committed position
Potentially:
M12
is processed again.
Therefore:
Rebalancing and at-least-once delivery naturally interact with duplicate processing.
Your consumer must therefore remain idempotent.
This is a very important connection.
________________________________________
30. The complete architecture
You should now be able to mentally model a scalable event system like this:
                         Producers
                      /      |      \
                     /       |       \
                    v        v        v
                +------------------------+
                |       Message Broker   |
                |                        |
                | Topic                  |
                |                        |
                | P0 P1 P2 P3 P4 P5      |
                +------------------------+
                  |  |  |  |  |  |
                  |  |  |  |  |  |
                  v  v  v  v  v  v
                +----------------------+
                | Consumer Group A     |
                | C1 C2 C3 C4 C5 C6    |
                +----------------------+

                +----------------------+
                | Consumer Group B     |
                | C1 C2 C3              |
                +----------------------+

                +----------------------+
                | Consumer Group C     |
                | C1 C2                |
                +----------------------+
And each partition is replicated:
P0
 |
 +── Broker A (leader)
 +── Broker B (replica)
 +── Broker C (replica)
So now you have:
Partitioning
    ↓
Parallelism

Consumer Groups
    ↓
Independent applications

Replication
    ↓
Fault tolerance

Offsets
    ↓
Recovery / replay

Idempotency
    ↓
Duplicate safety
________________________________________
31. Five rules to remember
Rule 1
Partition based on the entity whose ordering matters.
orderId → per-order ordering
accountId → per-account ordering
userId → per-user ordering
Rule 2
Partitions are the unit of ordered parallelism.
You can parallelize across partitions while preserving order inside each partition.
Rule 3
Consumer groups provide independent consumption.
Payment ≠ Analytics ≠ Notification
Each can maintain its own progress.
Rule 4
More partitions ≠ automatically more throughput.
Your consumers, CPU, database, downstream APIs, network, and broker capacity can all become bottlenecks.
Rule 5
Ordering doesn't eliminate duplicates.
A consumer can still process:
M1
M1
So at-least-once systems generally require idempotent processing.
________________________________________
32. Design Exercise — Food Delivery Events
Now solve this one yourself.
You have:
100,000 restaurants
10 million customers
Events:
OrderCreated
PaymentCompleted
RestaurantAccepted
FoodPrepared
