29. Kafka vs traditional queue
This distinction is worth memorizing.
Traditional Queue	Kafka
Message consumed → often removed	Record retained according to retention
Queue-centric	Log-centric
Consumer receives work	Consumer tracks position
Usually one consumer gets message	Multiple consumer groups can independently read
Ordering often queue-level	Ordering is partition-level
Replay often secondary	Replay is fundamental
Scaling via workers	Scaling via partitions + consumers
Kafka can absolutely be used for queue-like workloads, but its underlying abstraction is a distributed append-only log.
