Lesson 6 — Kafka Offset Management
This is one of the most important Kafka lessons.
If you understand offsets deeply, you'll understand why Kafka consumers can provide:
•	at-most-once
•	at-least-once
•	effectively-once patterns
•	duplicate processing
•	replay
•	crash recovery
•	consumer recovery after rebalance
The key idea is:
Kafka does not track "this message was processed." Kafka tracks a position in a partition.
________________________________________
1. First: What exactly is an offset?
Consider a partition:
P0

offset
  0 → Order A
  1 → Order B
  2 → Order C
  3 → Order D
  4 → Order E
The offset is simply the record's position in that partition.
Kafka doesn't use:
messageId = abc123
as the primary consumption position.
Instead:
partition + offset
identifies a record's position.
For example:
P0 + offset 3
means:
Order D
________________________________________
2. Offset is not global
This is critical.
Suppose:
P0:
0
1
2
3

P1:
0
1
2
3
There is no single global:
offset = 5
Instead:
P0 → current position = 3
P1 → current position = 2
Therefore a consumer group's progress is actually a vector of positions:
{
  P0: 3,
  P1: 2,
  P2: 17,
  P3: 91
}
This is why Kafka offsets are fundamentally partition-specific.
________________________________________
3. Three different things you must distinguish
This is the heart of today's lesson.
Suppose:
P0:

100
101
102
103
104
Your consumer has:
fetched position = 105
but your application has processed:
100
101
102
and committed:
102
There are multiple positions involved.
Conceptually:
             Kafka partition
                   │
                   ▼
100  101  102  103  104
      ↑         ↑
      │         │
 committed     fetched
More precisely, Kafka's consumer position is generally the next offset to fetch, while a committed offset is also a position from which processing should resume.
So after successfully processing offset 102, you'd normally commit:
103
meaning:
"On restart, resume from 103."
This is extremely important.
________________________________________
4. Why commit offset 103 after processing record 102?
Imagine:
P0:

100 → A
101 → B
102 → C
103 → D
You successfully process:
102 → C
You don't want to say:
committed = 102
because that can mean:
resume from 102
which could process C again.
Instead:
committed = 103
means:
100 ✓
101 ✓
102 ✓

resume → 103
So think:
Commit the next record you want to process.
________________________________________
5. The basic processing lifecycle
A reliable consumer conceptually does:
poll()
  ↓
record 100
  ↓
process(100)
  ↓
record 101
  ↓
process(101)
  ↓
commit offset 102
Meaning:
processed:
100, 101

resume from:
102
________________________________________
6. What does commit actually mean?
This is a common misconception.
When you commit an offset, Kafka is not marking the record itself as completed.
It is storing consumer progress.
Think:
commit(102)
as:
"For this consumer group and partition, my durable progress is at position 102."
Kafka doesn't know whether your database transaction succeeded.
It doesn't know whether:
email was sent
payment succeeded
database row was inserted
The application controls those semantics.
________________________________________
7. Where are committed offsets stored?
Kafka stores consumer group offsets in an internal Kafka topic:
__consumer_offsets
Conceptually:
Consumer
   |
   | commit
   ↓
Kafka group coordinator
   |
   ↓
__consumer_offsets
So consumer progress itself is stored durably inside Kafka.
This is an elegant design:
Kafka data
      +
Kafka-managed consumer state
________________________________________
8. Consumer restart
Suppose:
P0:

100
101
102
103
104
Consumer processes:
100 ✓
101 ✓
102 ✓
and commits:
103
Then:
💥 consumer crashes
When it restarts:
consumer
   ↓
group offsets
   ↓
103
It resumes from:
103
Therefore:
100 ✓
101 ✓
102 ✓
103 ← resume
________________________________________
9. The fundamental failure window
Now we get to the most important concept.
Suppose:
poll()
 ↓
process record 100
 ↓
process successful ✓
 ↓
CRASH
 ↓
commit never happened
Committed offset is still:
100
After restart:
resume from 100
So:
100
gets processed again.
This produces:
duplicate processing
This is the fundamental reason Kafka consumers often provide at-least-once processing.
________________________________________
10. The opposite failure window
Now change the order.
poll()
 ↓
commit offset
 ↓
process record
 ↓
CRASH
Suppose:
record = 100
commit = 101
Then:
process(100)
never completes.
After restart:
resume from 101
Record 100 is skipped.
Now you have:
message loss from the application's processing perspective
So:
process → commit
and:
commit → process
have very different failure semantics.
________________________________________
11. At-most-once
Pattern:
poll
 ↓
commit
 ↓
process
Failure:
commit ✓
process 💥
Record won't normally be retried.
Therefore:
at-most-once
means roughly:
A record is processed zero or one time, but processing can be lost.
________________________________________
12. At-least-once
Pattern:
poll
 ↓
process
 ↓
commit
Failure:
process ✓
commit 💥
After restart:
process again
Therefore:
at-least-once
means:
A record will be retried until progress is committed, but duplicates are possible.
This is the most common practical Kafka consumer model.
________________________________________
13. Visual comparison
At-most-once
record
 ↓
commit
 ↓
process
Failure:
commit ✓
process 💥

→ LOST
At-least-once
record
 ↓
process
 ↓
commit
Failure:
process ✓
commit 💥

→ RETRY
So:
at-most-once
    ↓
risk = loss

at-least-once
    ↓
risk = duplicate
This trade-off is fundamental.
________________________________________
14. enable.auto.commit
Kafka provides:
enable.auto.commit=true
This means the consumer automatically commits offsets periodically.
For example:
poll
 ↓
process
 ↓
poll
 ↓
process
 ↓
auto commit happens periodically
This is convenient.
But convenience can hide dangerous failure windows.
________________________________________
15. Why auto commit can surprise you
Suppose:
poll()
returns:
100
101
102
103
104
Your application processes:
100 ✓
101 ✓
but hasn't processed:
102
103
104
If the consumer commits its current position based on the records it has fetched, then a crash can cause some records to be skipped on restart.
The exact behavior depends on timing and consumer internals, but the architectural lesson is:
Don't assume "auto commit" means "commit after successful business processing."
It doesn't automatically know when your business operation succeeded.
________________________________________
16. Manual commit
For important business processing, you often use:
enable.auto.commit=false
Then your application explicitly controls commits.
Conceptually:
while (true) {
    ConsumerRecords<String, Order> records =
        consumer.poll(Duration.ofMillis(1000));

    for (ConsumerRecord<String, Order> record : records) {
        process(record);
    }

    consumer.commitSync();
}
Now the lifecycle is explicit:
poll
 ↓
process
 ↓
commit
This gives you much better control.
________________________________________
17. commitSync()
Example:
consumer.commitSync();
Conceptually:
Application
    |
    | commit request
    ↓
Kafka
    |
    | success/failure
    ↓
Application
The call waits for the commit operation to complete or fail.
Advantages:
simple
predictable
easy to reason about
Disadvantage:
adds synchronization / latency
For learning and correctness-sensitive paths, commitSync() is often the easier API to reason about.
________________________________________
18. commitAsync()
Example:
consumer.commitAsync();
The application doesn't wait in the same blocking way.
Conceptually:
Application
    |
    | commit request
    |-------------------->
    |
    ↓
continue processing
This can reduce latency overhead.
But now you have to reason about:
commit ordering
failures
callbacks
shutdown
It is more subtle.
________________________________________
19. Why asynchronous commits can be dangerous
Imagine:
commit A
commit B
and the network delays them.
You might conceptually end up with:
B arrives first
A arrives later
If older offsets overwrite newer progress, you can accidentally move the committed position backward.
Kafka's consumer commit APIs and their sequencing behavior are designed to address this, but the broader lesson remains:
Once you make offset commits asynchronous, you must reason carefully about ordering and failure.
A common production pattern is to use asynchronous commits during the normal loop but a synchronous commit during shutdown/rebalance handling.
________________________________________
20. Commit per record vs commit per batch
Suppose:
100 records
You can do:
process 1
commit
process 2
commit
...
That's potentially:
100 commits
Very expensive.
Instead:
poll 100
 ↓
process 100
 ↓
commit once
This is much more efficient.
So generally:
commit frequency
      ↕
duplicate window
      ↕
performance
________________________________________
21. Larger commit batches
Suppose:
1000 records
and commit every:
100 records
If a crash occurs after processing 99 records of a batch:
99 records
may be processed again.
So:
larger commit interval
means potentially:
larger duplicate window
but:
fewer commit operations
Again:
Performance and failure semantics are coupled.
________________________________________
22. Per-partition offsets
Suppose your poll returns:
P0:
100
101
102

P1:
200
201
You shouldn't think of one global offset:
offset = 103
Instead:
P0 → 103
P1 → 202
A commit can contain offsets for multiple partitions.
Conceptually:
{
    P0 → 103,
    P1 → 202
}
This is another reason Kafka's offset model is naturally partition-oriented.
________________________________________
23. Out-of-order processing creates an offset problem
Suppose:
P0:

100
101
102
You process concurrently:
Worker A → 100 → slow
Worker B → 101 → fast
Worker C → 102 → fast
Now:
102 ✓
101 ✓
100 still processing
Can you safely commit:
103
No—not if 100 has not completed.
Because committing 103 says:
100
101
102
are all safely past the restart point.
But:
100
isn't.
This is one of the reasons arbitrary parallel processing inside one partition complicates offset management.
________________________________________
24. The "contiguous prefix" principle
For ordered processing, think:
P0:

100 ✓
101 ✓
102 ✓
103 ✗
104 ✓
The safely committable position is:
103
not:
105
because:
103
hasn't completed.
Kafka offset commits represent a contiguous progress point.
This is a very useful distributed-systems concept.
________________________________________
25. Database + Kafka problem
Now we're entering real system design.
Suppose:
Kafka
  ↓
Consumer
  ↓
PostgreSQL
Business requirement:
Every Kafka order should be persisted exactly once in PostgreSQL.
Naive code:
process(record) {
    database.insert(order);

    consumer.commitSync();
}
Looks good.
But consider:
database.insert()
       ✓
       ↓
consumer.commitSync()
       💥
The DB write succeeded.
Kafka commit failed.
Restart:
same Kafka record
       ↓
database.insert()
       ↓
DUPLICATE
________________________________________
26. The opposite ordering is worse
Suppose:
consumer.commitSync();

database.insert(order);
Failure:
Kafka commit ✓
DB insert 💥
Restart:
Kafka says:
"already processed"
So the record isn't retried.
You lost the business operation.
Therefore:
Kafka offset + external DB transaction
cannot automatically become atomic just by changing two lines of code.
________________________________________
27. Idempotency becomes extremely important
A common solution is to make the business operation idempotent.
Suppose event has:
eventId = abc123
Database:
processed_events
----------------
abc123
Consumer receives:
abc123
First attempt:
insert eventId
→ success
perform business operation
Duplicate attempt:
insert eventId
→ already exists
→ skip
Now at-least-once Kafka delivery can be turned into:
effectively-once business behavior
assuming the idempotency mechanism itself is correctly designed.
________________________________________
28. Why "exactly once" is difficult
Suppose your system crosses:
Kafka
 ↓
Java application
 ↓
PostgreSQL
 ↓
HTTP payment provider
Can Kafka guarantee exactly-once across all of that?
No.
Kafka transactions can coordinate certain Kafka operations, but an external HTTP API is outside Kafka's transaction boundary.
This is a huge distributed-systems lesson:
Exactly-once is not a magical property that propagates across system boundaries.
We'll study Kafka transactions later.
________________________________________
29. Offset commit and rebalancing
Remember our previous lesson:
C1 → P0
C2 → P1
C2 crashes.
Partition P1 moves to C1.
C1 needs to know:
Where should I resume P1?
Answer:
committed offset for:
group + topic + partition
So consumer group state can be viewed as:
(groupId, topic, partition)
             ↓
       committed offset
For example:
(order-service, orders, P0) → 500
(order-service, orders, P1) → 731
(order-service, orders, P2) → 412
________________________________________
30. This is why consumer groups + offsets fit together
Consumer group answers:
Who owns the partition?
Offset answers:
Where should the new owner resume?
Together:
Consumer Group
      ↓
partition ownership
      ↓
consumer dies
      ↓
rebalance
      ↓
new consumer owns partition
      ↓
read committed offset
      ↓
resume
This is the complete failure-recovery mechanism.
________________________________________
31. Offset reset
What if there is no valid committed offset?
Kafka needs a policy.
Common configuration:
auto.offset.reset=earliest
or:
auto.offset.reset=latest
earliest
Start from the earliest available record.
P0:
100 101 102 103 104
↑
start here
latest
Start from the end of the available log.
P0:
100 101 102 103 104
                    ↑
                  start
So a new consumer group can either:
replay historical data
or:
only consume new data
depending on configuration and available offsets.
