________________________________________
43. The key lesson
Don't design:
RabbitMQ
   ↓
Consumer
   ↓
try/catch
Design a failure state machine:
                 ┌───────────────┐
                 │    MESSAGE    │
                 └───────┬───────┘
                         |
                      process
                         |
              ┌──────────┼───────────┐
              |          |           |
           success    transient   permanent
              |          |           |
             ACK       retry        DLQ
                         |
                      backoff
                         |
                      retry
                         |
                    max attempts?
                    /          \
                  no            yes
                  |              |
                retry           DLQ
And alongside it:
              MESSAGE
                 |
           idempotency key
                 |
          business operation
                 |
        idempotent result
That combination is the foundation of reliable RabbitMQ consumers.
________________________________________
What you should now know
You should be comfortable explaining:
ACK
  = "I successfully handled this delivery."

NACK(requeue=true)
  = "I failed; make it available again."

NACK(requeue=false)
  = "I failed; don't put it back into normal processing."

DLX
  = dead-letter exchange.

DLQ
  = queue where dead-lettered messages can be held.

Retry
  ≠ immediate requeue.

Retry
  = controlled re-attempt, ideally with backoff.

At-least-once
  = duplicates are possible.

Idempotency
  = application responsibility.
And the most important failure sequence:
Message
   ↓
Consumer processes successfully
   ↓
Consumer crashes BEFORE ACK
   ↓
RabbitMQ redelivers
   ↓
Business operation executes again
Therefore:
Reliable RabbitMQ system
=
at-least-once delivery
+
idempotent consumer
+
bounded retry
+
backoff
+
DLQ
+
observability
