25. Now make the broker fail
Suppose:
payment.q
is a quorum queue:
A = leader
B = follower
C = follower
Consumer connects through A.
Then:
A X
If B+C retain quorum:
B = new leader
C = follower
The consumer reconnects.
Messages that were properly replicated according to the queue's durability/consensus semantics can continue from the surviving quorum.
This is fundamentally different from a queue that existed only on A.
________________________________________
26. What does "accepted" mean?
This is a subtle interview question.
Someone asks:
"If RabbitMQ returned success, is the message guaranteed to survive a node failure?"
You should answer:
It depends on the publication/durability semantics and queue type.
You need to consider:
1. Was the message persistent?
2. Was the queue durable?
3. Was the queue replicated?
4. Were publisher confirms used?
5. Had the relevant replication/commit point been reached?
6. What failure occurred?
Don't answer:
RabbitMQ cluster = yes
That is too simplistic.
________________________________________
27. Availability vs durability
These are different.
Availability
Can I still publish/consume?
Durability
Will accepted data survive failures/restarts?
You can have:
available but not durable enough
or:
durable but temporarily unavailable
A good system-design answer explicitly separates them.
________________________________________
28. Three AZ architecture
Let's design a production deployment.
Suppose AWS:
AZ-A
AZ-B
AZ-C
Deploy:
RabbitMQ Node A → AZ-A
RabbitMQ Node B → AZ-B
RabbitMQ Node C → AZ-C
Then:
payment.q
  |
  +--> replica A
  +--> replica B
  +--> replica C
This is much stronger than:
Node A
Node B
Node C

all in AZ-A
because an entire AZ failure could take all replicas down.
________________________________________
29. Why physical failure domains matter
Suppose:
Node A
Node B
Node C
all run on:
same rack
and the rack loses power.
You lose:
3 / 3
despite having "three replicas."
Therefore:
Replication only protects against failures that are independent across the chosen failure domains.
For cloud architecture:
replicas
    ↓
different AZs
is much stronger.
For on-prem:
different racks
different power domains
different network paths
may matter.
________________________________________
30. Network partition vs node crash
These are different.
Node crash
A X
B and C can clearly observe A disappearing.
Network partition
A || B C
A might still be running.
It just cannot communicate with B/C.
Distributed systems find partitions harder because:
The failed node isn't necessarily dead.
It may think:
"I'm healthy."
while the majority thinks:
"We've lost A."
Consensus protocols exist partly to deal with this uncertainty.
________________________________________
31. Why quorum prevents two leaders
Suppose 3 nodes:
A B C
Partition:
A || B C
Could A elect itself leader?
It only has:
1 / 3
No quorum.
B+C have:
2 / 3
They can form quorum.
Therefore you don't get:
Leader A
+
Leader B
both legitimately committing conflicting state.
That's one of the major benefits of consensus.
________________________________________
32. But quorum has a cost
You don't get fault tolerance for free.
Replication means:
Producer
   |
   v
Leader
   |
   +---- network ----> follower
   |
   +---- network ----> follower
This introduces:
•	network traffic
•	storage writes
•	CPU
•	replication latency
•	recovery overhead
Therefore:
higher durability
      ↓
higher infrastructure cost
You choose based on business requirements.
________________________________________
33. RabbitMQ cluster ≠ load balancer
Another common misconception:
RabbitMQ Node A
RabbitMQ Node B
RabbitMQ Node C
doesn't automatically mean:
        Load Balancer
        /     |     \
       A      B      C
and every connection magically gets optimal queue locality.
You often need to think carefully about:
•	client connection endpoints
•	DNS/service discovery
•	load balancing
•	queue leader location
•	topology recovery
•	network paths
A connection is not simply "to the cluster"; it is established to a particular node.
________________________________________
34. Queue leader locality
Suppose:
payment.q leader = Node A
but your consumer connects to:
Node C
There can be additional network hops depending on topology and RabbitMQ behavior.
For high-throughput workloads, network topology and queue placement can therefore matter.
You should not assume:
3 nodes
=
everything is equally local
________________________________________
35. Failure recovery is not instantaneous
Suppose:
A = leader
A crashes
There is a sequence:
detect failure
     ↓
elect new leader
     ↓
re-establish replication
     ↓
clients reconnect
     ↓
consumers recover
     ↓
messages resume
During that period:
availability may temporarily degrade
This is why HA doesn't mean:
"Zero downtime under every failure."
It means the system is designed to recover while preserving specified guarantees.
________________________________________
36. Consumer redelivery after broker failure
Suppose:
M1 → Consumer
Consumer hasn't ACKed.
Broker fails/reconnect happens.
Depending on what state was durably committed and what happened during the failure, the message may be delivered again.
Therefore your application should already be prepared for:
redelivery
You don't build idempotency only because consumers crash.
You build it because distributed failure creates duplicate possibilities everywhere.
________________________________________
37. Exactly-once: be skeptical
If an interviewer says:
"Can RabbitMQ give me exactly-once processing?"
Don't confidently say:
YES
A better answer:
RabbitMQ provides mechanisms for reliable delivery and acknowledgements, but exactly-once business processing across RabbitMQ, a database, and external services is not something an ACK alone guarantees. In practice, use at-least-once delivery with idempotent processing and appropriate transactional patterns.
That's a much stronger distributed-systems answer.
________________________________________
38. Failure matrix
Let's build the mental table.
Failure	Likely consequence
Consumer crashes	Unacked messages can be redelivered
Consumer network disconnects	Connection/channels fail; recovery required
RabbitMQ node crashes, queue non-replicated	Queue availability depends on that node
RabbitMQ leader crashes, quorum remains	Another replica can become leader
Majority of quorum queue lost	Queue cannot safely make progress
Network partition, minority side	Cannot safely continue authoritative writes
Publisher connection fails before confirm	Producer doesn't know outcome
Producer retries uncertain publish	Duplicate publication possible
Consumer processes then ACK fails	Redelivery possible
Permanent poison message	Should eventually reach DLQ
