30. First naive design
Someone might create:
order.created.q
order.cancelled.q
order.shipped.q
payment.completed.q
...
and route each consumer separately.
It works.
But you're potentially creating a large number of queues.
More importantly, you're mixing two concepts:
event type
and:
consumer ownership
A queue should generally represent a consumer application's independent stream, rather than simply "one queue per event type."
________________________________________
31. Better design
Use an exchange:
events.exchange
with topic routing.
Then:
events.exchange
       |
       +----> payment.q
       |
       +----> inventory.q
       |
       +----> notification.q
       |
       +----> analytics.q
       |
       +----> shipping.q
Bindings:
payment.q
    order.created

inventory.q
    order.created
    order.cancelled

notification.q
    order.*
    
analytics.q
    #

shipping.q
    payment.completed
    inventory.reserved
Now the architecture maps nicely to consumer responsibilities.
________________________________________
32. Why analytics uses #
Analytics wants:
everything
So:
#
can conceptually bind all routing keys.
Then:
